    <DataNode elementId="AT3G30180-MONOMER" textLabel="Cytochrome P450 85A2" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q940V4" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">CYP85A2 is a multi-functional enzyme. First it was found to be a brassinosteroid C-6 oxidase |CITS: [12529536]|. Using a yeast transformation system, CYP85A2 was found to catalyze multiple steps in brassinosteroid biosynthesis, specifically C-6 oxidation of 6-deoxoteasterone to teasterone, 3-dehydro-6-deoxoteasterone to 3-dehydroteasterone, 6-deoxotyphasterol to typhasterol, and 6-deoxocastasterone to castasterone. CYP85A2 is a stronger C-6 oxidase than its homolog CYP85A1. In addition to be a brassinosteroid C-6 oxidase, CYP85A2 was shown to be a brassinolide synthase catalyzing the last lactonization step in brassinosteroid biosynthesis |CITS: [16024588] [15710611]|. In a most recent publication |CITS: [18685225]|, CYP85A2 was found to be able to convert teasterone and typhasterol to 7-ox-teasterone and 7-ox-typhasterol respectively. It indicated that CYP85A2 also catalyzes lactonization reactions of teasterone and typhasterol. Both 7-ox-teasterone and 7-ox-typhasterol were detected in Arabidopsis and were found more active than teasterone and typhasterol on stimulating hypocotyl elongation.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT3G30180-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT3G30180.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Interpro" value="IPR002401" />
