    <DataNode elementId="AT5G38970-MONOMER" textLabel="AT5G38970-MONOMER" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9FMA5" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The biosynthesis of brassinosteroids is branched two parallel pathways, the early and late C-6 oxidation pathways according to the C-6 oxidation status of the compounds. In &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt;, the C-6 oxidation of many brassinosteroids (but not all) is catalyzed by a steroid-6-oxidase encoded by the &lt;i&gt;AtBR6Ox&lt;/i&gt; gene. This protein has characteristics of cytochrome P450 (consensus heme-binding sequence, N-terminal hydrophobic anchor-domain to the endoplasmic reticulum) and is designated CYP85A1 |CITS:[11402205]|. This steroid -6-oxidase has a broad substrate specificity and, when expressed in yeast, can catalyze the conversion of 6-deoxoteasterone to teasterone, 3-dehydro-6-deoxoteasterone to 3-dehydroteasterone, 6-deoxothyphasterol to typhasterol and 6-deoxocastasterone to castasterone |CITS:[11402205]|. At the time of this review the enzyme has not been purified.</Comment>
